    "issued_at": "2026-09-01T05:15:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "610dcac8-5f13-462a-8e8d-396a60b96ba1",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 010515\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1015 PM MST Mon Aug 31 2026\n\n.UPDATE...06Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Chances for showers and thunderstorms will continue through\n  early this evening, mainly west of Phoenix, with Flood Watches \n  in effect. \n\n- Quiet weather under mostly clear skies is expected for Tuesday\n  and Wednesday before potentially another more active period of \n  weather materializes by the end of the week into the weekend.\n\n- Near to slightly below normal temperatures are expected\n  throughout much of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nLatest water vapor satellite imagery shows a well-defined upper-\nlevel shortwave over southern Nevada and is the main feature that\ndrove the widespread rainfall activity that was observed across \nmuch of south-central AZ throughout the morning hours. Rainfall \nintensity has been on the light to locally moderate side and thus \nrainfall accumulations for the most part have remained under 0.3\",\nwith some localized areas observing amounts up to 0.5\". Most of \nthis activity has moved east of Phoenix and will be ending in Gila\nCounty during the early afternoon hours. The overworked atmosphere\nfrom the morning activity will essentially end the potential for\nmore organized convective activity for the rest of today for\nmuch of south-central AZ. The main focus for convection for this \nafternoon will shift west of Phoenix, across portions of La Paz \ninto northern Yuma County as well as western Maricopa County as \nample heating will cause MLCAPE values to peak at 1500-2000 J/KG, \nwhich is more than sufficient for strong thunderstorms to develop.\nAlthough strong downburst wind gusts will be possible, the \nprimary hazard will be heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding \nwith localized amounts in excess of 1-2\" possible, especially with\nany training cells. As the upper-level shortwave lifts \nnortheastward into Utah by this evening, much of the upper-level \nsupport will be lifting to the north as well and thus activity \nshould diminish by the early evening hours with quieter conditions\nexpected during the late evening and overnight hours tonight.\n\nQuieter weather is expected Tuesday and Wednesday as significantly \ndrier air due to west-southwest mid-level flow will overspread the \nregion, especially in the mid to upper levels with strong subsidence \naloft. A few isolated storms will still be possible Tuesday \nafternoon, mainly across the terrain escarpments given that \nboundary level moisture will still be sufficient with mixing \nratios above 10 g/kg. However, by Wednesday, mixing ratios will \nhave dropped to less than 10 g/kg with little to no instability \nexpected and thus storm chances should be completely eliminated \nacross much of the CWA. \n\nDue to the abundant cloud cover and rainfall this morning, afternoon \nhigh temperatures across much of south-central AZ will likely \nstruggle to reach 90 degrees while significantly warmer highs due to \nmore sunshine can be expected across the western deserts as readings \ntop out in the mid to upper 100s. Temperatures Tuesday and \nWednesday, with much more sunshine, should rebound into the low 100s \nacross the south-central AZ lower deserts and top out into the mid \n100s across the western deserts, with these readings near to a few \ndegrees below normal for early September.  \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nForecast concern is increasing for the potential of tropical \nmoisture surging northward into the region as early as Thursday \nnight into Friday, but more so for the coming weekend. NHC shows a\ntropical disturbance currently near 10N and 107W with a 90% \nchance of cyclone formation this week and likely tracking \nnorthward off the coast of Baja by Friday into the weekend. There \nis still a lot of uncertainty with this potential tropical \nsystem, but model guidance has been coming to a bit better \nconsensus the last several model runs. Roughly 90% of the GEFS and\nEPS members now show PWATs increasing to 1.8-2.2\" over portions of \nour area by Friday or Saturday. Given this likely increase in \nmoisture, PoPs have gone up starting Friday and continue through \nthe weekend. It will likely be at least 2 or 3 more days until we \nhave a good idea on what would likely happen late this week. \nGetting tropical moisture into our region is definitely not \nunusual, but many times when it happens we struggle to build \ninstability due to extensive cloudiness and poor lapse rates. \nHowever, it seems quite likely a Pacific trough will be situated \nalong or just off the West Coast which may provide for enough \nupper level forced ascent to overcome the likely lackluster \ninstability. We will continue to watch this throughout the week as\nit very well could lead to another round of heavy rainfall at \nsome point.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0515Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period.\nWinds will return to following light and diurnal trends with\nvariability during directional shifts. There are hints of an\nisolated afternoon VCTS and later evening outflow, but current\nchances for either of these events is around 10% or less and\ntherefore have been excluded from the TAFs. Other than a FEW low-\nlevel bases around 7-10 kft, skies will be mostly clear.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \n\nNo major aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period.\nWinds will follow familiar diurnal trends with perhaps some\nvariability occurring during directional switches. Other than a\nFEW bases around 10 kft, skies will be mostly clear.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nLingering light showers will persist across the far eastern \ndistricts through early this afternoon with scattered showers and\nstorms across portions of southwest Arizona through early this \nevening. Drier conditions will move in beginning Tuesday with rain \nchances mostly ending through midweek. Expect MinRHs of 15-25% across the\nwestern districts to 35-50% over the eastern districts today \nbefore falling back to 15-25% areawide by Wednesday. Temperatures \nwill range from around normal over western districts to slightly \nbelow normal over the eastern districts.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...RW\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman/Lojero\n",
